  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n(Thursday Night - Sunday)\n\nThe cold front is expected to stall by Thursday night across \nsoutheast Missouri and southwest Illinois before pushing back \nnorthward early Friday. Chances of rain showers increase \nalong/poleward of the retreating boundary as low-level moisture \nconvergence strengthens and a weak midlevel shortwave moves out of \nthe south-central Plains. The exact placement of the west-east \nboundary varies slightly amongst deterministic guidance (~50 miles), \nbut the most likely location Friday afternoon is near the I-70 \ncorridor. This means that the best chances (50-70%) of light rain \nshowers on Friday are across northeast Missouri and west-central \nIllinois. The increased clouds/rain chances north of the front \nshould help create a north-south temperature gradient across the \nregion, with highs near 70 degrees across northeast Missouri and \nwest-central Illinois and closer to 80 degrees south of I-70. \n\nForecast uncertainty is higher for the weekend, as it now looks like \nindividual weak midlevel impulses will come across the Mississippi \nValley instead of one stronger shortwave trough. The track and \ntiming of each individual impulse is not certain, which is why there \nis a fairly long duration of chance (30-50%) PoPs this weekend. Any \nrain that does occur this weekend though does look light and \nshowery, with certainly more dry time than not this weekend. \nProbabilities for both measurable (>=0.01\") and 0.25\"+ amounts on \nthe LREF have dropped about 10-15% compared to this time yesterday. \nCooler high temperatures are expected regardless due to thicker \ncloud cover, with highs forecast to be in the upper 60s to low 70s.\n\n\n(Sunday Night - Tuesday)\n\nEnsemble guidance shows a deeper trough carving out across the \ndesert Sunday night into Monday. Cluster analyses of the 500-hPa \npattern diverge sharply by Tuesday with the track and timing of this \nfeature. Three of the four clusters show a closed, possibly cutoff, \nmid/upper level low forming across the central Plains. These \nclusters comprise 83% of the LREF membership, so the signal is \npretty strong. A closed mid/upper level low forming to our west \nshould lead to deep, anomalous southerly flow aloft. An increase in \ndeep-layer moisture along/ahead of a slow-moving frontal boundary is \na good recipe for a long-duration beneficial rain event across the \narea. Probabilities through Tuesday of at least 0.50\" of rain range \nfrom 65-90% from southeast to northwest. Those numbers jump even \nfurther if you go out through the middle of next week as the \npotential for a deep, closed low could portend to it impacting the \nregion for at least a few days.",
